<%# ── shared/uuid_badge ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    The ONE place a UUID is rendered (#1180).

    SPARC's object UUIDs are what the federation key grammar keys on, so an
    operator reading one off a screen is doing real work — and re-typing 36
    hex characters by eye is how a wrong identifier gets into a bundle. The
    badge is therefore a copy BUTTON, not text.

    Shared because #1180 puts this on the organization, boundary and five
    document screens. Repeating the markup seven times would mean seven places
    to keep the copy affordance, the accessible name and the theme pairing in
    step, and they would not stay in step.

    Locals:
      uuid:  (required) the identifier to show and copy
      label: optional prefix inside the badge, e.g. "UUID" — omit in a table
             column where the heading already says what it is
      name:  what the identifier IS, for the accessible name. Default "UUID";
             pass e.g. "boundary UUID" where the page shows more than one.
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── %>
<%
  uuid  = local_assigns[:uuid].to_s
  label = local_assigns.fetch(:label, nil)
  name  = local_assigns.fetch(:name, "UUID")
%>
<% if uuid.present? %>
  <span class="sparc-uuid" data-controller="clipboard" data-clipboard-text-value="<%= uuid %>">
    <button type="button"
            class="badge badge-identifier sparc-uuid__button"
            data-action="clipboard#copy"
            title="Copy <%= name %> to the clipboard">
      <% if label.present? %><span class="sparc-uuid__label"><%= label %></span><% end %>
      <span class="sparc-uuid__value"><%= uuid %></span>
      <%# Inline SVG, not an icon font: bootstrap-icons is not loaded in this
          app, so a `bi bi-clipboard` would render an invisible affordance.
          currentColor keeps it on the badge's own theme pairing. %>
      <svg class="sparc-uuid__icon" data-clipboard-target="icon" width="11" height="11"
           viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
           stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <rect x="5.5" y="5.5" width="9" height="9" rx="1.5"></rect>
        <path d="M11 3.5v-1a1 1 0 0 0-1-1H2.5a1 1 0 0 0-1 1V10a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h1"></path>
      </svg>
      <svg class="sparc-uuid__icon d-none" data-clipboard-target="check" width="11" height="11"
           viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"
           stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M2.5 8.5l4 4 7-9"></path>
      </svg>
      <span class="visually-hidden">Copy <%= name %> <%= uuid %> to the clipboard</span>
    </button>
    <%# The outcome is announced, not just shown: the only visual confirmation
        is a tick swap inside the button the user just pressed.

        <output> rather than a span with role="status" (Web:S6819). It carries
        the status role implicitly and is the element assistive technology is
        most consistently built against; the explicit role is not honoured
        everywhere. `for` is omitted deliberately — this announces the result of
        an action, not the computed output of named form fields. %>
    <output class="visually-hidden" data-clipboard-target="status"></output>
  </span>
<% end %>
